### What is the Java Platform Module System?

The Java Platform Module System, introduced in Java 9 under Project Jigsaw, is a language and runtime feature that groups packages into named, self-describing modules. Each module declares what it depends on and what it exposes. The compiler and JVM enforce these declarations, so internal packages stay truly hidden and missing dependencies fail at startup instead of at runtime.

```java
// module-info.java at the root of the com.bank.payments module
module com.bank.payments {
    requires com.bank.audit;           // dependency
    exports com.bank.payments.api;     // public surface
    opens com.bank.payments.model to com.fasterxml.jackson.databind; // reflection
}
```

```bash
# Compile a modular project
javac -d out --module-source-path src $(find src -name "*.java")

# Run a modular project
java --module-path out -m com.bank.payments/com.bank.payments.Main
```

### Why does JPMS exist?

Before Java 9 the unit of deployment was the JAR, and the classpath was a flat, unordered list. Any public class in any JAR could be used by any other code, so "public" meant "public to the entire world." There was no way to say "this package is my internal implementation." There was also the fragile-classpath problem: missing JARs were discovered lazily as `NoClassDefFoundError` deep into execution, and duplicate or conflicting versions silently shadowed each other.

- Before JPMS, Rajesh's payments team at National Bank of India exposed internal helper classes because `public` meant visible to everyone.
- Other teams reached into those helpers, and when the payments team tried to refactor, the entire bank's build broke.
- JPMS lets the payments team export only the API package and hide internals behind a module boundary.

```java
// Before JPMS: any code could use internal helpers
import com.bank.payments.internal.ValidationHelper; // worked on classpath

// After JPMS: this import fails at compile time because the package is not exported
// error: package com.bank.payments.internal is not visible
```

### How does module-info.java work?

A module is described by a `module-info.java` file at the root of its source tree, which compiles to `module-info.class`. The file lists directives that the compiler and JVM both enforce.

```java
// Full module descriptor for the bank's transfer service
module com.bank.transfer {
    // Dependencies
    requires com.bank.audit;                    // regular dependency
    requires transitive com.bank.model;         // re-exported to readers of this module
    requires static com.bank.annotations;       // compile-time only

    // Public surface
    exports com.bank.transfer.api;              // visible to all modules
    exports com.bank.transfer.spi to com.bank.reporting; // visible only to reporting

    // Reflection access
    opens com.bank.transfer.entity to org.hibernate.orm.core; // Hibernate can reflect

    // Service wiring
    provides com.bank.spi.FeeCalculator with com.bank.transfer.GstFeeCalculator;
    uses com.bank.spi.AuditSink;
}
```

```text
Directory layout:
com.bank.transfer/
├── module-info.java
└── com/bank/transfer/
    ├── api/
    │   └── TransferService.java      (exported)
    ├── spi/
    │   └── FeeCalculatorSpi.java     (qualified export)
    ├── entity/
    │   └── TransferEntity.java       (opened for reflection)
    └── internal/
        └── TransferValidator.java    (encapsulated)
```

### How do the requires, exports, and opens directives work?

The `requires` directive names a dependency. A special form `requires transitive` means that any module reading yours automatically also reads the transitive dependency. `requires static` means the dependency is needed at compile time but optional at run time, which is useful for annotation processors.

The `exports` directive makes a package part of the public API. Packages you do not export are strongly encapsulated. A qualified export like `exports com.bank.payments.internal to com.bank.reporting` reveals a package only to named friend modules.

The `opens` directive permits deep reflection. Under JPMS, `setAccessible(true)` on members of another module fails unless that package is opened.

```java
package com.bank.payments.api;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferService {
    /**
     * Transfer money between accounts at National Bank of India.
     * This class is in an exported package, so other modules can use it.
     */
    public BigDecimal transfer(String fromAccount, String toAccount, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Delegates to internal validator (not exported)
        return amount;
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.payments.internal;

// This class is in a non-exported package.
// Other modules CANNOT see it, even though it is public.
public class TransferValidator {
    public boolean validate(String accountId) {
        return accountId != null && accountId.startsWith("SAV");
    }
}
```

### How is JPMS different from the classpath?

| Feature | Classpath | JPMS |
|---|---|---|
| Encapsulation | None: all public classes visible | Strong: only exported packages visible |
| Dependency checking | Lazy: NoClassDefFoundError at runtime | Eager: validated at startup |
| Duplicate packages | Silently shadowed | Split-package rule rejects duplicates |
| Reflection | Unrestricted setAccessible | Requires opens directive |
| Custom runtimes | Full JRE required | jlink builds trimmed images |
| Unit of deployment | JAR (flat list) | Module (named, versioned, declared) |

```java
// On the classpath: reflection works on any class
import java.lang.reflect.Field;

public class ClasspathReflection {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // This worked on the classpath but fails under JPMS
        // unless the package is opened
        Class<?> clazz = Class.forName("com.bank.payments.internal.TransferValidator");
        Field[] fields = clazz.getDeclaredFields();
        for (Field f : fields) {
            f.setAccessible(true); // throws InaccessibleObjectException under JPMS
        }
    }
}
```

### What exceptions can occur with JPMS?

The most common exceptions are `IllegalAccessError` when code tries to use a non-exported package, and `InaccessibleObjectException` when reflection tries to access a non-opened package.

```java
import java.lang.reflect.Field;

public class ModuleReflectionError {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Attempting deep reflection into a non-opened package
        Class<?> clazz = Class.forName("com.bank.payments.internal.TransferValidator");
        Field field = clazz.getDeclaredField("maxAmount");

        // This throws java.lang.reflect.InaccessibleObjectException:
        // Unable to make field private java.math.BigDecimal
        // com.bank.payments.internal.TransferValidator.maxAmount
        // accessible: module com.bank.payments does not "opens
        // com.bank.payments.internal" to unnamed module
        field.setAccessible(true);
    }
}
```

```text
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.reflect.InaccessibleObjectException:
  Unable to make field private java.math.BigDecimal accessible:
  module com.bank.payments does not "opens com.bank.payments.internal"
  to unnamed module
```

To fix this, add `opens com.bank.payments.internal;` to the module descriptor, or use the command-line escape hatch:

```bash
java --add-opens com.bank.payments/com.bank.payments.internal=ALL-UNNAMED \
     -m com.bank.app/com.bank.app.Main
```

### What about ServiceLoader with provides and uses?

The service directives wire up the `ServiceLoader` mechanism with compiler-checked declarations, replacing the old `META-INF/services` text files.

```java
// Service interface in com.bank.spi module
package com.bank.spi;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public interface FeeCalculator {
    BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal transactionAmount);
}
```

```java
// Provider module declares the implementation
module com.bank.fees {
    requires com.bank.spi;
    provides com.bank.spi.FeeCalculator
        with com.bank.fees.GstFeeCalculator;
}
```

```java
// Consumer module loads the service
module com.bank.transfer {
    requires com.bank.spi;
    uses com.bank.spi.FeeCalculator;
}
```

```java
package com.bank.transfer.api;

import com.bank.spi.FeeCalculator;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ServiceLoader;

public class TransferService {
    private final FeeCalculator feeCalculator;

    public TransferService() {
        this.feeCalculator = ServiceLoader.load(FeeCalculator.class)
                .findFirst()
                .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalStateException("No FeeCalculator found"));
    }

    public BigDecimal transferWithFee(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        BigDecimal fee = feeCalculator.calculateFee(amount);
        BigDecimal total = amount.add(fee);
        System.out.println("Transfer " + amount + " + fee " + fee + " = " + total);
        return total;
    }
}
```

### What about jlink for custom runtimes?

The `jlink` tool builds a trimmed custom runtime image containing only the modules your application requires. This shrinks Docker containers dramatically.

```bash
# Build a custom runtime for the transfer service
jlink --module-path out:$JAVA_HOME/jmods \
      --add-modules com.bank.transfer \
      --output transfer-runtime \
      --strip-debug \
      --compress zip-6

# The result is often 40-60 MB instead of the 300+ MB full JDK
du -sh transfer-runtime/
# 48M  transfer-runtime/

# Run from the custom runtime
transfer-runtime/bin/java -m com.bank.transfer/com.bank.transfer.Main
```

```text
Docker image sizes:
  Full JDK base:      ~340 MB
  jlink custom image:  ~48 MB
  Savings:             ~86%
```

### What are common mistakes with JPMS?

The most common mistake is forgetting to open packages for frameworks that use reflection.

```java
// WRONG: Hibernate cannot reflect into entity package
module com.bank.app {
    requires org.hibernate.orm.core;
    exports com.bank.app.entity; // exports is not enough for reflection
}
```

```text
// Error at runtime:
// org.hibernate.MappingException: Could not get constructor for
// com.bank.app.entity.Account
```

```java
// CORRECT: open the package for deep reflection
module com.bank.app {
    requires org.hibernate.orm.core;
    exports com.bank.app.entity;
    opens com.bank.app.entity to org.hibernate.orm.core; // reflection allowed
}
```

Another mistake is the split-package problem, where two modules export the same package.

```text
// Error at startup:
// java.lang.module.ResolutionException:
// Modules com.bank.core and com.bank.legacy both export package
// com.bank.model to module com.bank.app
```

### When should you adopt JPMS?

JPMS pays off most for libraries and platforms that want a small, enforced public API and hidden internals. It also pays off for applications that want small `jlink` runtimes or use `ServiceLoader` plugins. For a shared library at National Bank of India where Pankaj wants other teams to use only the API package and never touch the internals, modules are exactly right.

### When should you NOT adopt JPMS?

For a typical Spring Boot microservice that runs as a fat JAR on the classpath, the cost usually outweighs the benefit. Reflective frameworks force many `opens` directives, some older libraries are not modular and must live as automatic modules, and the split-package rule can break assemblies that worked on the classpath. Many teams stay on the classpath and adopt JPMS selectively for shared libraries.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The JDK itself ships as about 90 platform modules; `java.base` is always present and does not need a requires directive.
- Automatic modules are JARs on the module path without a `module-info.java`; their name is derived from the JAR filename or the `Automatic-Module-Name` manifest entry.
- The unnamed module is where classpath code lives; it can read all named modules but named modules cannot require the unnamed module.
- The `--add-opens` and `--add-reads` flags are escape hatches for migration, not permanent solutions.
- Strong encapsulation of JDK internals means libraries that used `sun.*` classes now need `--add-opens` flags, which pushed libraries toward supported APIs.

```java
package com.bank.transfer.api;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Print the module this class belongs to
        Module mod = TransferDemo.class.getModule();
        System.out.println("Module name: " + mod.getName());
        System.out.println("Is named:    " + mod.isNamed());

        // List all packages exported by this module
        ModuleDescriptor desc = mod.getDescriptor();
        if (desc != null) {
            desc.exports().forEach(e ->
                System.out.println("Exports: " + e.source())
            );
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Module name: com.bank.transfer
Is named:    true
Exports: com.bank.transfer.api
```
